  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1)\n\nUpdate (315 AM) >\n\nAs visibilities have continued to deteriorate, a Dense Fog Advisory\nhas been issued for the entire ILN CWA through 10AM.\n\nPrevious discussion (259 AM) >\n\nCopious low level moisture is leading to widespread stratus\ndevelopment early this morning, along with some patchy dense fog. A\ndense fog advisory may be needed, though the stratus deck has been\nthe more consistent story so far, with surface visibilities varying\nfrom 1/4SM to 1SM.\n\nA surface cold front currently entering Michigan and Indiana will\nthen move southeast into the area this afternoon. As the ILN CWA \nremains well ahead of the front in the warm sector, temperatures will\nrise into the lower to upper 80s, producing 1500 to maybe 2500 J/kg\nof SBCAPE. Westerly wind flow aloft will be increasing going into the\nafternoon, with up to 50 knots at 500mb in the northern sections of\nthe ILN CWA. Though forcing with the front does not appear overly\nstrong, this combination will allow for a band of scattered storms to\ndevelop during the afternoon, primarily near or after 3PM. The\nparameter space supports a marginal severe threat, though extremely\npoor directional shear and somewhat limited storm coverage will\npreclude a more organized severe risk.\n\nTo note, the northwestern-most ILN counties (Wayne IN through Hardin\nOH) will likely remain dry, with storm initiation occurring after \nthe front has passed.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\n\nWith the frontal boundary well south of the region on Monday, and \nhigh pressure settling in over the Great Lakes, dry conditions are \nexpected for the ILN CWA on Monday. This will also be the coolest day\nof the period, with highs only in the 70s for most of the forecast \narea.\n\nA larger shift to the weather pattern is expected to begin Tuesday,\nwith strong ridging (or even a closed high) setting up over the \nsoutheastern states. The main storm track and frontal zone will\nremain north of the ILN CWA to begin on Tuesday, but will shift\nsouthward as the ridge breaks down. Not out of the question that some\nstorms could get into the northern ILN CWA on Tuesday afternoon and\nevening, though chances will increase on Wednesday and Thursday as\nthe overall frontal zone (and the mid-level ridge) both collapse\nslightly southward. For locations that remain south of the storms and\nthe front, temperatures may get quite warm, with highs in the mid \n80s to near 90 on Tuesday, Wednesday, and thursday.",
